WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:15.769
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here, and I promise I’m
not here to beat a dead tauntaun.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:20.480
As I’m sure everyone knows, The Last Jedi
pissed off a lot of fans.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.390
At Wisecrack, we’ve surfaced the usual rants:
Luke Skywalker is suddenly a green milk-chugging

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.390
coward.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.260
Snoke gets Darth Maul-ed before we know anything
about him.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.829
Holdo’s plan doesn’t make any sense, the
Canto Bight section is boring, Rey’s parents

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:39.879
were a red herring, Marvel humor for days
— "Hi, I'm holding for General Hugs?”

00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:42.829
“This is Hux.” — and of course, Leia
Poppins.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:45.729
But we’re not gonna spend 15 minutes fanboy
raging.

00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:50.550
Instead, we’re going to discuss how Rian
Johnson tried to revolutionize Star Wars,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.550
but didn’t.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:52.710
So what went wrong?

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.460
Well to answer that we’ll need a little
help from a guy who, in a roundabout way,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.579
is responsible for making Stars Wars Star
Wars.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:01.109
Nope, not that guy.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:02.140
This guy.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.519
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Last
Jedi: What Went Wrong?

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.270
And Spoiler alert, even though I already spoiled
pretty much everything.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:14.369
Now, before we jump in, if you’ll allow
me to get a little meta on you.

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:18.630
The relationship between Kylo Ren and Darth
Vader can be interpreted as a commentary on

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.070
the relationship new Star Wars has with the
original Star Wars.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.540
“That you will never be as strong as Darth
Vader."

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.450
In The Force Awakens, Kylo Ren is burdened
by the task of living up to the legend of

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.409
his grandfather, just as the new series has
the seemingly impossible pressure of living

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:37.899
up to the legacy of the original trilogy.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:42.570
Just as Kylo Ren mimics Darth Vader by dressing
like like him and using a voice modulator,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.389
even though there’s nothing wrong with his
voice, — “Look how old you’ve become.”

00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:49.869
— so, too, does The Force Awakens mimic
A New Hope.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:54.130
As many critics have noticed, it’s almost
beat per beat the same movie.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.780
This might seem like a stretch, but the dynamic
between Snoke and Kylo in opening of The Last

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.810
Jedi furthers this meta reading, as Snoke
tells Kylo to take off the stupid mask — “Take

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.369
that ridiculous thing off.”

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.500
— and that he shouldn’t even bother anymore.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.400
“Alas, you're no Vader.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.299
You're just a child.”

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:15.299
And then?

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:16.769
Ren breaks the mask.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:19.930
They might as well go and throw Luke’s lightsaber
off a cliff.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.400
Oh wait…

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.760
Johnson is telegraphing to his audience that
this will no longer be a rehash of the original

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.760
trilogy.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.099
The film will be a radical subversion of the
Star Wars formula.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:34.050
For the new Star Wars to live, the old Star
Wars must die.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.470
"Let the past die.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:39.070
Kill it if you have to."

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.379
With the Last Jedi, Rian Johnson isn’t interested
in recycling Old Star Wars.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:45.430
He’s interested in progress.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.170
Not only progressing the franchise itself,
but the landscape of a certain galaxy far,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.170
far away.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.790
In order to progress the franchise, Johnson
abrasively undermines the tropes and traditions

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:58.250
that characterized the previous films.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.860
Whereas Luke Skywalker was the son of noble
blood, “Oh, Luke!"

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.450
Rey’s parents are just degenerate winos.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:05.760
"They were nobody."

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.939
“They were filthy junk traders.

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:10.060
Sold you off for drinking money."

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:15.220
Our heroes are now janitors and mechanics
instead of politicians and princesses.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:20.780
Luke is no longer the idealistic hero, he’s
a failed nephew murder/slovenian philosopher.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:26.740
Yoda goes from symbol of Jedi piety to would-be
book burner, and the Jedi order kinda sucks.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:27.740
“Lesson two.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.659
Now that they’re extinct, The Jedi are romanticized.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:30.659
Deified.

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:31.909
But if you strip away the myth and look at
their deeds…

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:38.450
The legacy of the jedi is failure.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.700
Hypocrisy.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:40.700
Hubris.”

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.920
“That’s not true” “At the height of
their powers they allowed Darth Sidious to

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.830
rise, create the empire and wipe them out.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:51.719
It was a jedi master who was responsible for
the training and creation of Darth Vader.”

00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:55.670
So you might be asking: Why sh*t on everything
that defines the previous films?

00:03:55.670 --> 00:04:00.250
Well, cuz turns out, the events of the previous
films didn’t really lead to any progress.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:05.799
After the collapse of the Empire a nearly
identical Nazi-inspired fascistic order has

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:06.799
risen.

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:09.730
So, clearly something is wrong in the galaxy
if this keeps happening.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.720
So, what’s to blame for this re-emergence
of evil?

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.140
Well, Johnson seems to suggest it’s our
inadequate conception of good and evil.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.140
But wait!

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.570
Stark divisions between good and evil are
what the entire Star Wars mythology has been

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:22.570
built around.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.030
It’s the Dark SIDE not the Dark “nuance.”

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.400
"Only a master of evil, Darth."

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.980
And here’s where the movie almost gets really
smart.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:35.320
As many of you may know, George Lucas crafted
the original trilogy point for point around

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.130
a book called “The Hero with a Thousand
Faces.”

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.230
Written by Joseph Campbell, the book sets
forth a kind of universal story structure

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:47.350
from which myths, stories and literature all
borrow - which is why the structure is called

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:48.610
“the monomyth.”

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.080
But, instead simply recycling Campbell’s
Monomyth as seen in the original trilogy,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.810
Johnson uses it to complicate our relationship
to the dark side.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:01.140
You see, Campbell’s monomyth is based on
the work of psychologist Carl Jung, who wrote

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.670
about ideas like the collective unconscious
and “archetypes,” universal “primordial”

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.190
images ingrained deep in our psyche.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:12.080
Those archetypes, for Campbell, inform the
kind of characters that keep popping up in

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.480
stories across continents and cultures.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.070
So, let’s talk about one of those archetypes:
the shadow, and how The Last Jedi tries to

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.850
radically redefine our relationship to it.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:26.061
For Jung, the shadow is the primal or animal
part of our unconscious that tends to get

00:05:26.061 --> 00:05:27.700
repressed - the “dark side.”

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.620
See where we’re going with this?

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.480
In the monomyth, this dark side usually gets
embodied by a character who represents the

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.620
shortcomings, fears, and anxieties of our
protagonist.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.460
Gollum is Frodo’s shadow, a stark reminder
of what he could become.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.690
But, if you really want “Shadows 101”
- look no further than Darth Vader to Luke

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:46.690
Skywalker.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.930
Darth Vader was once a powerful Jedi who succumbed
to the Dark Side.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:54.160
For Luke, Darth Vader serves as a constant
reminder of what he could become if he does

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.010
not conquer his fear and master his passions.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:02.100
So, confronting Darth Vader is more than just
a physical confrontation, it’s an emotional

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.030
and psychological one - it’s why Luke spares
Darth Vader’s life.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:10.740
If he were to strike him down in anger, then
he’s lost the battle with his inner demons.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.030
But whereas the old Star Wars wants you to
conquer your inner demons, the new Star Wars

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.470
has other ideas.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:21.060
In all the films, we're meant to believe that
balance in the force is desirable for the

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.100
good of the galaxy.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.830
"You refer to the prophecy of the one who
will bring balance to the force.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:28.800
You believe it's this boy?"

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.030
However, The Last Jedi suggests that the force
is something that inherently promotes balance.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:39.230
When the darkness rises, the force amplifies
the light to balance it out.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:45.710
"Darkness rises and light to meet it.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:52.720
I warned my young apprentice that as he grew
stronger, his equal in the light would rise."

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.460
If we're to believe Snoke, here, wouldn't
that mean the inverse is true?

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:02.020
That any powerful Jedi order would be met
by an equally powerful dark side?

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.900
If so, we have no choice but to read Luke's
criticism of the Jedi order as one that blames

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.430
them, in part, for the cyclical rise and fall
of these fascist empires.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.050
While it's kind of ambiguous, this logical
conclusion seems to be hinted at by Luke.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:19.190
“And this is the lesson.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.990
That force does not belong to the jedi.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:27.320
To say that if the jedi die, the light dies,
is vanity.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.320
Can you feel that?”

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.340
With Episode 8, the dark side is no longer
something to be conquered by the valiant Jedi

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:37.880
at the behest of the light side, because that
will make the force "overcorrect" to achieve

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.410
balance, leading to more amplfiications of
evil.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:46.460
According to Jungian analyst, Robert A. Johnson
— NOT To be confused with RIAN Johnson.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:50.720
— we shouldn’t think of our “dark sides”
as something to merely conquer and expunge.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:55.131
Johnson says this act of repression causes
us to “accumulate the darkness” which

00:07:55.131 --> 00:07:57.210
creates an “ego split.”

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.830
According to his reading of Jung, when you
suppress the darkness, you’re forced to

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:06.830
find a scapegoat, project all your own ugliness
on to them, leading to a never-ending fight.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:11.970
This is quite literally done in the very way
the original Star Wars is written - with Luke’s

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.620
inner demons projected onto an evil space
wizard.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:20.330
Instead of maintaining this classic distinction
between good and bad, both within our characters

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.680
and in the general plot, Rian Johnson tries
to complicate it at every step of the way.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.680
As we see, conquering evil with good hasn’t
done much for the galaxy.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.469
The empire is basically back with a sexy new
rebranding, and Snoke is basically Palapatine

00:08:34.469 --> 00:08:35.500
2.0.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:40.810
In Earlier Star Wars the good/bad distinction
is literally black and white, and thus The

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:43.780
Force Awakens brings us right back to where
we started.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:48.660
So to progress the franchise, Rian Johnson
tweaks the basic Jung-ish formula.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.389
If Jung suggested that we must reconcile our
dark side with the rest of our personality,

00:08:53.389 --> 00:08:58.220
does the galaxy need to reconcile its dark
side with the larger order of things?

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.220
"Balance.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.240
Powerful light, powerful darkness."

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.100
Consider Benicio del Toro’s character DJ.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.800
Rather than upholding the traditional distinction
between purely good Rebels and purely evil

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:14.490
oppressors, he explores how even the good
guys can get caught up with the darker elements

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:15.560
of the universe.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:20.949
As the Galaxy’s very own Blackwater mercenary,
the cynical DJ sees through the false divisions

00:09:20.949 --> 00:09:21.949
of “good” and evil.”

00:09:21.949 --> 00:09:26.550
"At least you’re stealing from the bad guys
and helping the good."

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:27.550
“Good guys?

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:28.550
Bad guys?

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:30.230
Made up words.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:35.389
Let’s see who formerly owned this gorgeous
hunk.

00:09:35.389 --> 00:09:41.040
Ah, this guy was an arms dealer.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.490
Made his bank selling weapons to the bad guys.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.480
Oh… and the good.”

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:54.410
This marks the most morally ambiguous moment
in any Star Wars film when he not only suggests

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:59.670
that divisions between good and evil are insincere
and unhelpful, but complicates our understanding

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.240
of the rebellion as a infallible good.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.889
Robert Johnson sees our traditional understanding
of the light and dark sides as straight up

00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:12.399
dangerous: he says that our inability to reconcile
with our shadow, and therefore our eventual

00:10:12.399 --> 00:10:17.800
scapegoating onto others, will inevitably
end in violence, war, and racism.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:23.010
For the Last Jedi, rather than the good guys
and bad guys, there’s a system at play.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.480
"It’s all a machine, partner.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.870
Live free.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:28.870
Don't join."

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:34.180
It becomes the first Star Wars film to explicitly
critique the galaxy’s military economy,

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:39.170
and the exploitation and animal rights violations
inherent in it, which even heroes like Princess

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:40.519
Leia are party to.

00:10:40.519 --> 00:10:42.579
"Then shelled us to test their weapons."

00:10:42.579 --> 00:10:47.800
So, you might be asking at this point: aside
from being too ballsy, What went wrong?

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:52.959
Well, the movie goes out of its way to, in
both form and content, tell us that we must

00:10:52.959 --> 00:10:58.129
kill the past - whether it be the past structure
of Star Wars films, or the moral lens we understood

00:10:58.129 --> 00:10:59.820
the previous films through.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.640
Then this happens: "It’s time to let old
things die.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:04.670
Snoke.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:05.709
Skywalker.

00:11:05.709 --> 00:11:07.779
The Sith.

00:11:07.779 --> 00:11:09.840
The Jedi.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:11.910
The rebels.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:16.050
Let it all die.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:17.080
Rey.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:21.230
I want you to join me."

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:26.889
Rey is given a chance to end all the dogmatism
of the previous films and yet… she says

00:11:26.889 --> 00:11:28.040
no.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.670
And from this point on, it’s all backpedal
central.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:31.670
"No, no.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.279
You're still holding on!

00:11:33.279 --> 00:11:34.279
Let go!”

00:11:34.279 --> 00:11:38.160
All the things that we were convinced need
to be killed are reaffirmed.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.089
Tearing down the heroic archetypes inherent
in the monomyth?

00:11:41.089 --> 00:11:42.889
“The temple was burning.

00:11:42.889 --> 00:11:53.510
He had vanished with a handful of my students
and slaughtered the rest.

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:59.410
Leia blamed Snoke, but it was me.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.089
I failed.

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:08.309
Because I was Luke Skywalker, Jedi Master.

00:12:08.309 --> 00:12:18.620
A legend.“ The legend of Luke Skywalker
lives on, "Luke Skywalker," inspiring the

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:23.290
galaxy once again and this time as an added
bonus: it’s a lie!

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.290
Yay!

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.540
Necessity to tear down established orders
that delineate the division between good and

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:28.540
evil?

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:29.600
"It’s time for the Jedi to die."

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:30.600
Nope.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:35.240
“And I will not be the last Jedi.”

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:38.680
Desire to distance itself from the established
narrative structure of the original trilogy?

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:39.680
Nope.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:42.310
“Strike me down in anger, and I’ll always
be with you.”

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:47.699
“If you strike me down, I shall become more
powerful than you can possibly imagine.”

00:12:47.699 --> 00:12:48.920
Forget the Jedi past?

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:49.920
Nope.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:50.920
Save the books.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.660
It’s not that movies can’t insert ideas
and then ultimately reject them.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.060
But usually the ideological shift happens
as the plot pushes our heroes to reconsider

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:02.480
their ideas in interesting and thought-provoking
ways.

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:08.160
Instead, Luke decides to reaffirm all the
antiquated ideas because… he loves his sister?

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:09.259
I guess?

00:13:09.259 --> 00:13:15.029
Other reversals are equally perplexing: don’t
be a hero, "She was more interested in preserving

00:13:15.029 --> 00:13:21.860
the light than she was seeming like a hero,"
until it’s ok to be a hero?

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:27.100
And by this point, the film has made a psychologically
sophisticated case for why Rey should team

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.100
up with Ren: the old way doesn’t work.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:34.779
Jedis are repressed as hell, and we’re stuck
playing a galactic game of Nazi Whack-a-Mole.

00:13:34.779 --> 00:13:35.810
Fine.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.470
We need a radical change.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:40.480
Which is literally what Ren seems to be offering.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:44.980
Despite her faith in Ren’s essential goodness,
and despite Luke’s lesson that the force

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.500
beyond is good and evil, Rey hesitates.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:52.439
Here’s where Rey and Ren become opposing
stand-ins for the film’s two potential moral

00:13:52.439 --> 00:13:58.290
codes: Ren is the realist who knows that the
Rebels will try to impose the same moral expectations

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:00.790
onto Rey as they did to Luke.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:05.350
Having become well acquainted with his bad
side and his good, and wisely calling out

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:10.750
the folly of the false division, Ren is arguably
closer to finding that Jungian balance than

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:11.750
most.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:16.589
He seems to be offering to Rey exactly what
Robert Johnson and Jung say a person should

00:14:16.589 --> 00:14:22.670
strive for: recognition that the ego and shadow
are both part of a person, and what’s more,

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.570
that one literally cannot exist without the
other.

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:30.579
Meanwhile, Rey represents the old moral code
of Star Wars: the temptation to run away with

00:14:30.579 --> 00:14:37.079
her swole new crush is ultimately overcome
by her desire to be the good Jedi, a morally-pure,

00:14:37.079 --> 00:14:43.510
infallible hero who definitely isn’t suppressing
a lifetime’s supply of darkness, no siree!

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:46.889
So, if you’re going to revolutionize Star
Wars, great!

00:14:46.889 --> 00:14:49.899
Want to reframe how we think about the central
conflict?

00:14:49.899 --> 00:14:50.899
Cool!

00:14:50.899 --> 00:14:55.559
But spending all this time abrasively subverting
everything we love and know about Star Wars

00:14:55.559 --> 00:14:59.910
just to shrug and revert back to where we
started: less cool.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:01.519
But what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:15:01.519 --> 00:15:07.089
Is this latest iteration of Star Wars sacrilegious
or a much-needed fresh take on the series?

00:15:07.089 --> 00:15:22.290
Let us know in 

00:15:22.290 --> 00:16:07.569
the comments.

